00:40:25.622 --> 00:40:26.989
with the children, yeah.

00:40:26.989 --> 00:40:29.362
Yeah, she had a short fuse

00:40:29.362 --> 00:40:31.534
and a very pregnant belly.

00:40:31.534 --> 00:40:32.367
Yeah.

00:40:32.367 --> 00:40:33.200
It's like, I...

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:34.478
In retrospect, I kind of feel for-

00:40:34.478 --> 00:40:35.311
Oh yeah. Could you imagine?

00:40:35.311 --> 00:40:36.144
But now I was like...

00:40:36.144 --> 00:40:37.112
I was like, "What is
wrong with this woman?

00:40:37.112 --> 00:40:39.871
I mean, I know that she's
pregnant, but I mean,

00:40:39.871 --> 00:40:41.153
what's her problem?"

00:40:41.153 --> 00:40:41.986
She was a-
Now I'm like-

00:40:41.986 --> 00:40:43.042
Good teacher though.
Good gosh.

00:40:43.042 --> 00:40:44.679
She was a good teacher.

00:40:44.679 --> 00:40:46.416
Learned a lot of math in that class.

00:40:46.416 --> 00:40:47.409
Yeah.

00:40:47.409 --> 00:40:50.658
And then in six grade I was
in Ms. Campbell's class.

00:40:50.658 --> 00:40:52.611
And I was in Ms. Leneer's class.

00:40:52.611 --> 00:40:53.552
Ms. Leneer's class.

00:40:53.552 --> 00:40:54.385
And at that point-

00:40:54.385 --> 00:40:56.604
I really feel like I missed
out on Ms. Leneer's class

00:40:56.604 --> 00:40:58.237
but Ms. Campbell was super sweet.

00:40:58.237 --> 00:40:59.478
It was a combination class.

00:40:59.478 --> 00:41:00.788
So there was some...

00:41:00.788 --> 00:41:02.197
It was the smart sixth graders

00:41:02.197 --> 00:41:04.552
and the not as smart seventh graders.

00:41:04.552 --> 00:41:05.385
Yeah, right.

00:41:05.385 --> 00:41:06.218
That's yeah.

00:41:07.086 --> 00:41:08.157
That's really how it worked.

00:41:08.157 --> 00:41:09.333
Yeah, it's pretty simple.

00:41:09.333 --> 00:41:10.595
You know, it's...

00:41:10.595 --> 00:41:11.994
We haven't really thought
about it in a while

00:41:11.994 --> 00:41:13.545
that like, every year...

00:41:13.545 --> 00:41:16.950
We met in first grade
then every other year

00:41:16.950 --> 00:41:18.900
we weren't in the same class.

00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:19.899
Yeah.

00:41:19.899 --> 00:41:23.685
So, and you have this memory
of like, from the beginning

00:41:23.685 --> 00:41:26.158
going on sleepovers to people's homes,

00:41:26.158 --> 00:41:28.105
in the Book of Mythicality,

00:41:28.105 --> 00:41:30.390
if you're into this type of conversation

00:41:30.390 --> 00:41:33.415
and you haven't gotten your
copy of the Book of Mythicality

00:41:33.415 --> 00:41:34.870
get one because there's stories

00:41:34.870 --> 00:41:36.533
that we're not gonna share here

00:41:36.533 --> 00:41:41.533
and lots of other stories from
our shared best friendship,

00:41:42.027 --> 00:41:44.047
even though now we're dismantling it.

00:41:44.931 --> 00:41:47.948
Available in audio book
form as well that we read.

00:41:47.948 --> 00:41:51.449
Oh, probably even better.

00:41:51.449 --> 00:41:54.492
I don't remember spending a night.

00:41:54.492 --> 00:41:55.859
I remember I told the story

00:41:55.859 --> 00:41:57.705
spending the night at Zach West's house

00:41:57.705 --> 00:41:59.469
and then he pile drived me.

00:41:59.469 --> 00:42:00.691
Mm.

00:42:00.691 --> 00:42:04.367
And he wasn't best friend material,

00:42:04.367 --> 00:42:05.717
is how I put it in the book.

00:42:05.717 --> 00:42:06.550
Right.

00:42:06.550 --> 00:42:10.530
I think I spent the night at
Matthew Enzor's house once

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:14.530
and his dad, Benny, was
watching Alien and like,

00:42:16.375 --> 00:42:17.960
I felt really uncomfortable.

00:42:21.574 --> 00:42:22.407
So yeah.

00:42:22.407 --> 00:42:23.240
It's just like-
I don't know, man.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:24.923
I just remember feeling uncomfortable

00:42:24.923 --> 00:42:26.074
in other environments.

00:42:26.074 --> 00:42:29.499
Like, I was only comfortable at home

00:42:29.499 --> 00:42:33.130
so I can believe that if
you invited yourself over

00:42:33.130 --> 00:42:35.019
to my house, I wasn't gonna say no,

00:42:35.019 --> 00:42:36.803
I would be like, "Sure."

00:42:36.803 --> 00:42:40.658
But I would not have been
comfortable going to your house

00:42:40.658 --> 00:42:41.974
at such an early age.

00:42:41.974 --> 00:42:43.989
Oh, I didn't invite people to my house

00:42:43.989 --> 00:42:45.501
'cause I already know about that.

00:42:45.501 --> 00:42:46.334
Okay, so-

00:42:46.334 --> 00:42:49.659
I didn't have sleepovers
at my house until...

00:42:49.659 --> 00:42:51.400
Like, very few of these people.

00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:53.181
Probably sixth grade, maybe seventh grade.

00:42:53.181 --> 00:42:54.070
Yeah.

00:42:54.070 --> 00:42:55.522
So, okay my-

00:42:55.522 --> 00:42:57.045
And that's when I would've
started spending the night

00:42:57.045 --> 00:42:57.878
at your house.

00:42:57.878 --> 00:43:01.131
My perspective on this is that...

00:43:01.131 --> 00:43:04.623
Again, the way that my brain works

00:43:04.623 --> 00:43:06.123
is that I don't...

00:43:08.102 --> 00:43:11.450
Especially at the time, I
didn't like categorize people,

00:43:11.450 --> 00:43:12.301
right.

00:43:12.301 --> 00:43:14.732
And so if you asked me in first grade,

00:43:14.732 --> 00:43:15.565
"Who's your best friend?"

00:43:15.565 --> 00:43:16.443
I'd be like,

00:43:16.443 --> 00:43:17.939
"I got lots of friends",

00:43:17.939 --> 00:43:22.443
and I would probably answer
that question the same way.

00:43:22.443 --> 00:43:24.289
I would say that it
was probably like third

00:43:24.289 --> 00:43:27.440
and fourth grade after
Ben moved into town.

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:28.273
Yeah.

00:43:28.273 --> 00:43:30.856
It was probably, especially that year,

00:43:30.856 --> 00:43:34.248
the Ms. Everhart year where
we were not in the same class.

00:43:34.248 --> 00:43:35.081
Yeah.

00:43:35.081 --> 00:43:36.903
Where I would've said
Ben was my best friend

00:43:36.903 --> 00:43:38.926
if you would've put me up against a wall.

00:43:38.926 --> 00:43:40.957
But I did not think in terms.

00:43:40.957 --> 00:43:43.311
Maybe not and there's a
reason I'll give in a minute

00:43:43.311 --> 00:43:44.519
but-
I didn't think in terms of-

00:43:44.519 --> 00:43:45.352
I'm not surprised by this, yeah.

00:43:45.352 --> 00:43:46.185
I makes sense.

00:43:46.185 --> 00:43:48.192
I didn't think in terms of that,

00:43:48.192 --> 00:43:50.319
it was more just like

00:43:50.319 --> 00:43:52.718
there's people that I like
to hang out with a whole lot.

00:43:52.718 --> 00:43:54.161
But everybody, like...

00:43:54.161 --> 00:43:56.397
If I mention any guy,

00:43:56.397 --> 00:43:58.241
tell me if you spend
the night at his house,

00:43:58.241 --> 00:43:59.305
Adam.

00:43:59.305 --> 00:44:00.568
Yeah.

00:44:00.568 --> 00:44:01.401
David R.

00:44:01.401 --> 00:44:02.234
Yes.

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:05.192
You didn't...

00:44:05.192 --> 00:44:06.794
Let's see who,

00:44:06.794 --> 00:44:11.226
the Enzor's, did you spend
Matthew Enzor's house?

00:44:11.226 --> 00:44:12.059
Not...

00:44:12.059 --> 00:44:13.656
Definitely at least a couple of times

00:44:13.656 --> 00:44:14.688
but I don't remember exactly.

00:44:14.688 --> 00:44:17.147
It may have been a little bit later.

00:44:17.147 --> 00:44:17.980
Tate Maddox.

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:20.147
Oh yeah, multiple times.

00:44:21.863 --> 00:44:22.780
Who else?

00:44:24.469 --> 00:44:25.831
Zach.

00:44:25.831 --> 00:44:27.003
You spent a night at Zach's house?

00:44:27.003 --> 00:44:27.836
Oh yeah.

00:44:27.836 --> 00:44:28.805
Julian.

00:44:28.805 --> 00:44:30.094
Oh, Julian, skateboarder?

00:44:30.094 --> 00:44:30.927
Yeah.

00:44:30.927 --> 00:44:32.242
Oh, he was too intimidating.

00:44:32.242 --> 00:44:33.422
And I think Chris too.

00:44:33.422 --> 00:44:34.255
Chris Morris?

00:44:34.255 --> 00:44:35.338
I think so.

00:44:36.456 --> 00:44:37.506
Of course, Peter.

00:44:37.506 --> 00:44:39.605
Peter Dinklage, not the Peter Dinklage.

00:44:39.605 --> 00:44:41.656
Well, he was your neighbor,
so you'd walk over.

00:44:41.656 --> 00:44:43.036
He was younger too.

00:44:43.036 --> 00:44:45.152
You would spend a night at
somebody a year younger?

00:44:45.152 --> 00:44:47.895
You got a house, you got
a fridge, you got a TV,

00:44:47.895 --> 00:44:48.728
I'm there.

00:44:49.619 --> 00:44:50.638
So you would-
Justine.

00:44:50.638 --> 00:44:51.854
Oh, Justin?

00:44:51.854 --> 00:44:52.687
Mm-hm.

00:44:52.687 --> 00:44:54.641
You'd go around the
corner to Justin's house?

00:44:54.641 --> 00:44:58.558
Every kid in my neighborhood
was on the list.

00:45:00.017 --> 00:45:01.571
Michael Jubi, he was in the mix.

00:45:01.571 --> 00:45:04.271
Oh yes, Michael Jubi had the best house.

00:45:04.271 --> 00:45:05.214
He had that-

00:45:05.214 --> 00:45:06.364
Had a loft with a spiral staircase.

00:45:06.364 --> 00:45:08.822
Loft thing with a spiral staircase.

00:45:08.822 --> 00:45:11.035
And he was on a golf course.

00:45:11.035 --> 00:45:11.868
Oh yeah.

00:45:11.868 --> 00:45:15.270
Whole 13 at the time on Keith Hills, man.

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:17.610
And his parents were fun to talk to.

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:19.080
His parents were so cool.

00:45:19.080 --> 00:45:20.951
So cool.
Yeah, man.

00:45:20.951 --> 00:45:22.288
Let's see.

00:45:22.288 --> 00:45:24.204
I didn't spend the night at
any of these people's houses

00:45:25.385 --> 00:45:26.218
ever.

00:45:27.065 --> 00:45:28.516
I was gregarious, man.

00:45:28.516 --> 00:45:29.690
I was up for a good time.

00:45:29.690 --> 00:45:30.918
That...

00:45:30.918 --> 00:45:33.378
When Adam Nicholson had that
sleepover for his birthday

00:45:33.378 --> 00:45:35.015
and we watched Texas Chainsaw Massacre,

00:45:35.015 --> 00:45:37.053
that may have been the
first time I spent the night

00:45:37.053 --> 00:45:40.153
at anyone's house and that
was probably seventh grade,

00:45:40.153 --> 00:45:41.406
sixth grade.

00:45:41.406 --> 00:45:42.239
Really?

00:45:42.239 --> 00:45:43.623
Yeah.

00:45:43.623 --> 00:45:44.456
I mean-

00:45:44.456 --> 00:45:46.209
I can name 10 more, by the way, if you...

00:45:46.209 --> 00:45:48.081
I need a yearbook.

00:45:48.081 --> 00:45:49.265
Okay.

00:45:49.265 --> 00:45:51.577
We won't get into it.

00:45:51.577 --> 00:45:52.636
You know, Brooks.

00:45:52.636 --> 00:45:53.469
I don't...

00:45:53.469 --> 00:45:55.417
Okay, so Brooks was younger too.

00:45:55.417 --> 00:45:56.793
Two years younger, but we were-

00:45:56.793 --> 00:45:57.626
But he was a family friend.

00:45:57.626 --> 00:45:58.459
We were...

00:45:58.459 --> 00:45:59.499
Our families were really close.

00:45:59.499 --> 00:46:00.332
And what...

00:46:00.332 --> 00:46:01.346
So, and you loved it.

00:46:01.346 --> 00:46:03.163
You were never like, "Get me outta here"?

00:46:03.163 --> 00:46:04.090
No, man.

00:46:04.090 --> 00:46:05.231
I...

00:46:05.231 --> 00:46:06.627
Here's the thing, I-

00:46:06.627 --> 00:46:09.100
I mean, we're so different in this way.

00:46:09.100 --> 00:46:09.933
But this is something-

00:46:09.933 --> 00:46:11.431
But it didn't register, that's the thing.

00:46:11.431 --> 00:46:13.103
No, but this is something that

00:46:13.103 --> 00:46:14.183
I didn't know about myself

00:46:14.183 --> 00:46:16.410
and I've never really thought about it,

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:19.835
but like I, especially back in the day,

00:46:19.835 --> 00:46:21.857
I don't feel like I do
this as much anymore,

00:46:21.857 --> 00:46:25.034
but like I would find the
way to connect with somebody.

00:46:25.034 --> 00:46:27.062
I would be like, this is
the thing that they're into

00:46:27.062 --> 00:46:28.877
and it's just like, I'm into everything

00:46:28.877 --> 00:46:30.153
so when I go to this person's house

00:46:30.153 --> 00:46:31.697
all we talk about is this.

00:46:31.697 --> 00:46:32.530
And you know-

00:46:32.530 --> 00:46:33.619
I didn't dictate what we talked about.

00:46:33.619 --> 00:46:35.116
It was like this person's into this,

00:46:35.116 --> 00:46:37.655
"This person's really into Nintendo,

00:46:37.655 --> 00:46:40.053
well, I can be really into Nintendo."

00:46:40.053 --> 00:46:41.807
You spent the night at
Chad Leander's house?

00:46:41.807 --> 00:46:42.640
Yes.

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:44.209
And just played Nintendo,

00:46:44.209 --> 00:46:46.235
just like he would do a
speed run through it all?

00:46:46.235 --> 00:46:47.406
I think it was one...

00:46:47.406 --> 00:46:49.191
I think one time.

00:46:49.191 --> 00:46:50.024
Okay.

00:46:50.024 --> 00:46:50.857
But...

00:46:50.857 --> 00:46:52.043
And it's funny because
now that I've learned more

00:46:52.043 --> 00:46:54.543
about the Enneagram, you know,

00:46:56.046 --> 00:46:58.567
one of the things they
call the Enneagram three

00:46:58.567 --> 00:46:59.832
is the chameleon, right.

00:46:59.832 --> 00:47:00.686
Hm.

00:47:00.686 --> 00:47:01.900
It's a person who-

00:47:01.900 --> 00:47:02.733
Blends in.

00:47:02.733 --> 00:47:05.832
Can enter into a environment
and immediately be like,

00:47:05.832 --> 00:47:07.943
"This is what we're doing here?

00:47:07.943 --> 00:47:09.804
All right, this is what I'm doing.

00:47:09.804 --> 00:47:11.413
If that's what makes everybody happy,

00:47:11.413 --> 00:47:12.358
that's what I'm gonna do."

00:47:12.358 --> 00:47:13.812
And that brought me joy.

00:47:13.812 --> 00:47:18.075
And you had a really
stable home life, you know,

00:47:18.075 --> 00:47:19.934
I think, and it...

00:47:19.934 --> 00:47:23.256
I'm doing some work now to
kind of go back to that point

00:47:23.256 --> 00:47:25.106
and this is kind of helpful.

00:47:25.106 --> 00:47:26.784
Like, I'm not gonna bring it up here

00:47:26.784 --> 00:47:29.699
like, I'm not gonna bring the
therapy component into this

00:47:29.699 --> 00:47:31.044
except to say...

00:47:31.044 --> 00:47:33.456
Maybe later but for now
the only thing I'll say

00:47:33.456 --> 00:47:34.882
is that like, yeah,

00:47:34.882 --> 00:47:39.369
I'm coming to grips with the
fact that like, you know,

00:47:39.369 --> 00:47:41.702
my mom did an excellent job.

00:47:42.747 --> 00:47:45.447
I'm not critiquing anything
she did with the hand

00:47:45.447 --> 00:47:47.989
that was dealt her but it was not...

00:47:47.989 --> 00:47:49.996
I had this perception of it,

00:47:49.996 --> 00:47:54.302
it was not a stable environment, you know?

00:47:54.302 --> 00:47:57.635
And I had this really keen sense of like

00:47:59.113 --> 00:48:03.863
just a gut feeling of lack of
safety outside of the home.

00:48:07.304 --> 00:48:08.508
That's an oversimplification

00:48:08.508 --> 00:48:11.025
and maybe an over dramatization of it,

00:48:11.025 --> 00:48:11.858
but it was like

00:48:11.858 --> 00:48:14.767
that's kind of how I
ventured into the world

00:48:14.767 --> 00:48:16.481
was with trepidation.

00:48:16.481 --> 00:48:17.314
Yeah.

00:48:17.314 --> 00:48:18.220
So, you know,

00:48:18.220 --> 00:48:20.618
when you were spending the
night at people's homes,

00:48:20.618 --> 00:48:24.950
like, what was I doing? I was
doing my own thing at home

00:48:24.950 --> 00:48:28.790
like watching this new
thing called Nickelodeon

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:30.353
on channel 34.

00:48:30.353 --> 00:48:31.186
I didn't have cable,

00:48:31.186 --> 00:48:32.091
that was another reason

00:48:32.091 --> 00:48:33.222
I had to get to other people's homes,

00:48:33.222 --> 00:48:34.131
they had cable.

00:48:34.131 --> 00:48:37.659
And then I would do some
exploring in the woods

00:48:37.659 --> 00:48:40.992
and all the stuff around my house alone.

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:47.063
I would pretend to be an
army guy who was like-

00:48:47.063 --> 00:48:47.896
Yeah.

00:48:47.896 --> 00:48:48.729
And like a commando.
And we-

00:48:48.729 --> 00:48:49.719
And we had the-

00:48:49.719 --> 00:48:52.310
So you remember those duck
boots that everybody had

00:48:52.310 --> 00:48:54.762
and on the back of them
they said "Explorers"?

00:48:54.762 --> 00:48:55.690
Uh-huh.

00:48:55.690 --> 00:48:58.177
Because I think it was like
an addition of that duck boot.

00:48:58.177 --> 00:48:59.010
Yeah.

00:48:59.010 --> 00:49:02.050
So Cole and I had those
and then Jeremy Fisher

00:49:02.050 --> 00:49:05.725
down the street had those boots as well,

00:49:05.725 --> 00:49:09.170
and so we were the explorers
but that was again,

00:49:09.170 --> 00:49:10.823
that was with-

00:49:10.823 --> 00:49:11.656
Neighborhood kids.

00:49:11.656 --> 00:49:13.823
Yeah, that was my older
brother, three years older.

00:49:13.823 --> 00:49:15.109
I Didn't have any-
And so we would go-

00:49:15.109 --> 00:49:16.531
Neighborhood kids either.

00:49:16.531 --> 00:49:17.827
Like name the forks and stuff like that

00:49:17.827 --> 00:49:18.660
but like.

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:19.769
My community wasn't like that,

00:49:19.769 --> 00:49:21.811
we we're on opposite sides of the town.

00:49:21.811 --> 00:49:22.644
Right.

00:49:22.644 --> 00:49:24.166
So...

00:49:24.166 --> 00:49:28.758
But I think one of the main
forces that brought us together

00:49:28.758 --> 00:49:30.758
and it superseded school

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:35.909
and then might change a little
bit of the complexion of like

00:49:35.909 --> 00:49:39.573
what would've been in your
mind as a fourth grader saying

00:49:39.573 --> 00:49:41.624
that Ben was your best friend,

00:49:41.624 --> 00:49:44.835
which you certainly would've said,

00:49:44.835 --> 00:49:49.578
but would've kind of put me in
that conversation was church.

00:49:49.578 --> 00:49:51.498
Yeah.

00:49:51.498 --> 00:49:54.764
You know, from the moment you moved there

00:49:54.764 --> 00:49:57.364
you joined Buies Creek
First Baptist Church.

00:49:57.364 --> 00:50:02.006
The church that me and mom and
my stepdad, Jimmy, went to.

00:50:02.006 --> 00:50:04.021
So we were at that church.

00:50:04.021 --> 00:50:07.560
Why do you think your
parents chose that church?

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:12.302
I think Jimmy had a
connection to that church

00:50:12.302 --> 00:50:15.480
that went further back, so
it was like there was like

00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:18.988
a longer established connection there,

00:50:18.988 --> 00:50:20.299
but...

00:50:20.299 --> 00:50:22.132
And then I was very...

00:50:23.208 --> 00:50:24.405
We were both very involved.

00:50:24.405 --> 00:50:27.102
Your parents were very involved.

00:50:27.102 --> 00:50:30.962
My mom, if she could pull
everything together in time

00:50:30.962 --> 00:50:32.324
to get to church on time.

00:50:32.324 --> 00:50:33.157
Yeah.

00:50:33.157 --> 00:50:35.813
She was gonna be there but she, you know,

00:50:35.813 --> 00:50:38.813
she had a hard time getting ready

00:50:38.813 --> 00:50:40.877
to the point where at a
certain point I'd be like,

00:50:40.877 --> 00:50:42.892
"Mom, you need to stop halfway

00:50:42.892 --> 00:50:46.162
in your like, makeup and hair routine

00:50:46.162 --> 00:50:49.597
to bring me to church because
the kids are making fun of me

00:50:49.597 --> 00:50:52.144
for being late for church
every Sunday morning

00:50:52.144 --> 00:50:53.539
for Sunday school."

00:50:53.539 --> 00:50:55.566
And then she would come back and like

00:50:55.566 --> 00:50:58.235
show up halfway through
the Sunday school lesson

00:50:58.235 --> 00:50:59.932
or just show up at church.

00:50:59.932 --> 00:51:01.583
She could not get herself ready.

00:51:01.583 --> 00:51:02.750
Wow.

00:51:04.096 --> 00:51:06.781
But yeah, we would Sunday school

00:51:06.781 --> 00:51:09.119
and church every Sunday morning,

00:51:09.119 --> 00:51:11.135
every Wednesday night there'd be something

00:51:11.135 --> 00:51:12.360
and then there was usually

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:16.337
another night of the week
that we were at church.

00:51:16.337 --> 00:51:17.261
So it was like,

00:51:17.261 --> 00:51:21.305
even the years that we
weren't in the same class

00:51:21.305 --> 00:51:24.888
we were spending like
our youth group time-

00:51:25.790 --> 00:51:26.623
Yeah, that's true.

00:51:26.623 --> 00:51:29.177
And church time together and like,

00:51:29.177 --> 00:51:33.313
so, and we were very close in that world.

00:51:33.313 --> 00:51:35.462
Like, if you think of everybody
else that went to church...

00:51:35.462 --> 00:51:37.155
Who were the other guys
that went to church with us?

00:51:37.155 --> 00:51:38.044
There wasn't...

00:51:38.044 --> 00:51:40.697
Like, Matthew McKinney
went to church with us

00:51:40.697 --> 00:51:43.292
And Matthew and John.

00:51:43.292 --> 00:51:45.547
Matthew, and John Enzor.

00:51:45.547 --> 00:51:48.442
But, so it was a smaller group that like

00:51:48.442 --> 00:51:50.775
we were the closest to each.

00:51:51.651 --> 00:51:52.484
Yeah, yeah.

00:51:52.484 --> 00:51:53.406
But by that time I would've-

00:51:53.406 --> 00:51:54.239
And so...

00:51:54.239 --> 00:51:55.305
And that was from an early time.

00:51:55.305 --> 00:51:57.839
And by third grade...

00:51:57.839 --> 00:52:00.856
Third, fourth, fifth, sixth grade

00:52:00.856 --> 00:52:03.603
I would not have said that
any of those other kids

00:52:03.603 --> 00:52:05.335
that I had spent the night
with were my best friends.

00:52:05.335 --> 00:52:06.168
Yeah.

00:52:06.168 --> 00:52:07.897
I would be like, my
friends are Ben and Link.

00:52:07.897 --> 00:52:08.730
Yeah.

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:10.239
In terms of like my friends my age

00:52:10.239 --> 00:52:11.318
and then I would be...

00:52:11.318 --> 00:52:12.847
And I think I would've been like,

00:52:12.847 --> 00:52:14.685
and I hang out with Brooks
quite a bit too, but yeah.

00:52:14.685 --> 00:52:15.518
Sports...

00:52:15.518 --> 00:52:16.351
Yeah, when you talk about Brooks.

00:52:16.351 --> 00:52:18.221
Sports was such a big part
of your like, basketball.

00:52:18.221 --> 00:52:19.575
Yeah.

00:52:19.575 --> 00:52:21.313
Like, you played everything,
you played baseball,

00:52:21.313 --> 00:52:22.496
you played soccer.

00:52:22.496 --> 00:52:24.551
I played soccer from...

00:52:24.551 --> 00:52:26.023
That was the big thing in Buies Creek,

00:52:26.023 --> 00:52:26.856
everybody...

00:52:26.856 --> 00:52:30.193
That was like a five, six
and seven year old team,

00:52:30.193 --> 00:52:31.321
recreation team.

00:52:31.321 --> 00:52:32.745
Like, they started you really young.

00:52:32.745 --> 00:52:34.425
It was like very organized

00:52:34.425 --> 00:52:37.493
the what, basketball
recreation didn't exist.

00:52:37.493 --> 00:52:38.326
Yeah.

00:52:38.326 --> 00:52:39.209
Baseball recreation didn't exist.

00:52:39.209 --> 00:52:41.608
But like, it was interesting
that soccer was the thing

00:52:41.608 --> 00:52:46.297
that was like really exploding
in like the late eighties.

00:52:46.297 --> 00:52:47.130
And so-

00:52:47.130 --> 00:52:48.585
And Campbell had a really good team.

00:52:48.585 --> 00:52:49.734
That's right.

00:52:49.734 --> 00:52:50.980
So like,

00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:55.044
I always played soccer
and had enough confidence

00:52:55.044 --> 00:52:59.627
to at times, begin to enjoy
it but not any other sports

00:53:00.597 --> 00:53:02.381
but you were into every sport.

00:53:02.381 --> 00:53:04.871
So we didn't connect in that way

00:53:04.871 --> 00:53:09.039
but I played all the other
sports all the way through,

00:53:09.039 --> 00:53:10.872
you know, up until...

00:53:12.731 --> 00:53:16.736
Yeah, like baseball started
at like maybe sixth grade.

00:53:16.736 --> 00:53:17.569
Mm-hm.

00:53:17.569 --> 00:53:19.571
And I did play, boy, I hated that,

00:53:19.571 --> 00:53:20.485
maybe fifth grade.

00:53:20.485 --> 00:53:21.318
Yeah.

00:53:21.318 --> 00:53:22.151
But...

00:53:22.151 --> 00:53:25.802
So a little bit of soccer
but it was mainly church

00:53:25.802 --> 00:53:28.948
and like your parents
were so committed to it,

00:53:28.948 --> 00:53:31.666
and I kind of folded
in underneath that like

00:53:31.666 --> 00:53:34.501
level of commitment that
was like, I was, I mean,

00:53:34.501 --> 00:53:36.704
as we've established from a young age,

00:53:36.704 --> 00:53:39.014
you followed your parents' example

00:53:39.014 --> 00:53:41.597
and I kind of followed
your family's example

00:53:41.597 --> 00:53:43.291
of like being super committed.

00:53:43.291 --> 00:53:44.124
Yeah.

00:53:44.124 --> 00:53:46.658
It was like that was
from an very early age,

00:53:46.658 --> 00:53:48.927
our identity as people was-

00:53:48.927 --> 00:53:49.893
Right.

00:53:49.893 --> 00:53:52.567
Our evangelical belief system,

00:53:52.567 --> 00:53:56.550
even in contrast to the
other church across town,

00:53:56.550 --> 00:53:58.640
Memorial Baptist and say,

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:01.200
"Oh, they're the more liberal church"

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:04.782
and like, we had friends from school

00:54:04.782 --> 00:54:07.413
who went to that church but
we thought of them differently

00:54:07.413 --> 00:54:08.854
because it wasn't...

00:54:08.854 --> 00:54:10.104
They weren't as

00:54:12.180 --> 00:54:15.363
fervent in our opinion as we were.

00:54:15.363 --> 00:54:17.750
I mean, that's a whole
story in and of itself

00:54:17.750 --> 00:54:18.634
is just, yeah,

00:54:18.634 --> 00:54:20.641
there's a saying for that,

00:54:20.641 --> 00:54:22.252
there's something of minor differences

00:54:22.252 --> 00:54:23.085
I can't remember what it is,

00:54:23.085 --> 00:54:26.054
but it says that, "You've
got to have an enemy"

00:54:26.054 --> 00:54:29.714
and in this little teeny tiny town

00:54:29.714 --> 00:54:32.445
there's two main churches
that are two Baptist churches

00:54:32.445 --> 00:54:34.933
and those two Baptist
churches have to find a way

00:54:34.933 --> 00:54:36.023
to look down on one
another, it's just so crazy

00:54:36.023 --> 00:54:38.482
that that's the way humans work.

00:54:38.482 --> 00:54:39.521
Because there were...

00:54:39.521 --> 00:54:40.749
I mean, read the Lost
Causes of Bleak Creek

00:54:40.749 --> 00:54:45.242
you'll see how he folded
that into the novel, but...

00:54:45.242 --> 00:54:47.763
And there weren't many people,

00:54:47.763 --> 00:54:52.152
we didn't have many classmates
who didn't go to one church

00:54:52.152 --> 00:54:54.792
or the other, you know, so it was really,

00:54:54.792 --> 00:54:56.148
that's why there was such a dichotomy.

00:54:56.148 --> 00:54:58.334
It's like everybody went
to one church or the other.

00:54:58.334 --> 00:55:01.229
And we were getting closer
to the group of kids

00:55:01.229 --> 00:55:04.425
that were our age that
were at church as well.

00:55:04.425 --> 00:55:06.092
And as we got older,

00:55:07.590 --> 00:55:11.335
you know, you start
taking it more seriously.

00:55:11.335 --> 00:55:12.168
Oh yeah.

00:55:12.168 --> 00:55:14.295
And so then the fact that
you take this very seriously

00:55:14.295 --> 00:55:17.957
and like, you believe in God
in this very particular way

00:55:17.957 --> 00:55:19.502
and you practice that belief

00:55:19.502 --> 00:55:21.435
then you start making
connections with people.

00:55:21.435 --> 00:55:22.268
Right.

00:55:22.268 --> 00:55:23.727
And also there's like the whole like,

00:55:23.727 --> 00:55:25.706
you start kind of drawing
some political lines

00:55:25.706 --> 00:55:28.005
because that's part of it as well

00:55:28.005 --> 00:55:32.327
and of course we're talking early nineties

00:55:32.327 --> 00:55:35.219
south, by that time, the Christian right

00:55:35.219 --> 00:55:37.818
had pretty much taken hold
in the conservative party-

00:55:37.818 --> 00:55:38.651
Mm-hm.

00:55:38.651 --> 00:55:40.416
So republicans, so it was just like,

00:55:40.416 --> 00:55:41.256
"Oh, are you a Democrat or Republican?"

00:55:41.256 --> 00:55:42.089
Right.

00:55:42.089 --> 00:55:45.857
So it's like you needed to
be a Republican Christian

00:55:45.857 --> 00:55:46.937
in order to be like,

00:55:46.937 --> 00:55:49.078
really in on the in club
and that was beginning...

00:55:49.078 --> 00:55:50.916
That was starting to filter in the way

00:55:50.916 --> 00:55:52.580
that we were thinking, right.

00:55:52.580 --> 00:55:54.040
Yeah.

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:55.112
And I remember even, you
know, around like fifth,

00:55:55.112 --> 00:55:57.811
sixth grade, Ben started
to kind of experience

00:55:57.811 --> 00:55:59.811
some of the issues that-

00:56:02.445 --> 00:56:06.079
The tension 'cause he
went to Memorial Baptist.

00:56:06.079 --> 00:56:09.692
Well, but also he was always
such a open-minded like-

00:56:09.692 --> 00:56:10.525
Yeah.

00:56:10.525 --> 00:56:12.213
Challenging thinker, his parents were too.

00:56:12.213 --> 00:56:13.184
'Cause his family was.

00:56:13.184 --> 00:56:14.943
And so it's just like that started

00:56:14.943 --> 00:56:15.870
to get a little complicated

00:56:15.870 --> 00:56:18.070
as I'm like getting serious about this,

00:56:18.070 --> 00:56:19.140
it's kind of like.

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:21.064
Yeah, it's like your
dad was a law professor.

00:56:21.064 --> 00:56:21.935
He's not really into this.

00:56:21.935 --> 00:56:23.078
He's got more questions about this.

00:56:23.078 --> 00:56:24.803
But Ben's dad was a
biology professor, right?

00:56:24.803 --> 00:56:25.720
Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:56:25.720 --> 00:56:26.553
So it's like, you know,

00:56:26.553 --> 00:56:28.829
you can definitely see that vibe.

00:56:28.829 --> 00:56:29.662
Right.

00:56:29.662 --> 00:56:31.031
Of like, you know,

00:56:31.031 --> 00:56:34.172
the rule following versus the-

00:56:34.172 --> 00:56:35.700
Well, but coupled with the fact that

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:39.268
Ben was also beginning to
experience some emotional things

00:56:39.268 --> 00:56:40.451
and some physical things.
Right.

00:56:40.451 --> 00:56:42.731
Like trying to figure out
has he got chronic fatigue

00:56:42.731 --> 00:56:43.564
whatever,

00:56:43.564 --> 00:56:44.952
I can't remember exactly when that started

00:56:44.952 --> 00:56:49.307
to manifest itself, but
that began to complicate.

00:56:49.307 --> 00:56:51.332
It's difficult for kids to understand

00:56:51.332 --> 00:56:53.552
how to navigate a friendship
when someone's going through

00:56:53.552 --> 00:56:54.527
stuff like that.

00:56:54.527 --> 00:56:55.420
Which again,

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:59.055
we'll refer to the other
podcast that, you know,

00:56:59.055 --> 00:57:03.010
where we talked at length about
Ben, our other best friend,

00:57:03.010 --> 00:57:04.295
so we don't have to
get back into that now.

00:57:04.295 --> 00:57:07.962
But I mean, we certainly
connected over like

00:57:09.000 --> 00:57:10.565
being out in nature,

00:57:10.565 --> 00:57:13.036
so even though you spend a lot
of time playing basketball,

00:57:13.036 --> 00:57:17.043
as we got into these
older elementary grades,

00:57:17.043 --> 00:57:18.376
like you were...

00:57:19.485 --> 00:57:24.288
I was more comfortable coming
out of my shell, you know,

00:57:24.288 --> 00:57:27.675
gramping up into middle school and like

00:57:27.675 --> 00:57:28.508
starting to get like...

00:57:28.508 --> 00:57:29.761
We connected over like being in nature

00:57:29.761 --> 00:57:33.105
and I got in on what
you and Ben were doing.

00:57:33.105 --> 00:57:35.274
But back to the church thing, I mean,

00:57:35.274 --> 00:57:39.024
our first teacher, I
remember was Ms. Tolsma.

00:57:40.338 --> 00:57:42.256
Tosome, something like that.

00:57:42.256 --> 00:57:43.089
She-

00:57:43.089 --> 00:57:44.018
I do not remember her.

00:57:44.018 --> 00:57:45.624
She was my neighbor across this street

00:57:45.624 --> 00:57:47.932
and she was our Sunday
school teacher at fist.

00:57:47.932 --> 00:57:49.170
Was she blonde and had all the kids?

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:51.377
She had the two twins who were blonde.

00:57:51.377 --> 00:57:52.210
The blonde twins.

00:57:52.210 --> 00:57:53.561
Oh good gosh.

00:57:53.561 --> 00:57:54.394
Really?

00:57:54.394 --> 00:57:55.727
The fantasies,

00:57:56.745 --> 00:57:57.690
the fantasies

00:57:57.690 --> 00:57:59.573
of just going over there and swinging

00:57:59.573 --> 00:58:02.141
on the swing set with those blonde twins.

00:58:02.141 --> 00:58:02.974
Yeah.

00:58:02.974 --> 00:58:04.226
That's all I wanted.
I Didn't remember.

00:58:04.226 --> 00:58:05.059
I just wanted to swing with them.

00:58:05.059 --> 00:58:06.887
I didn't remember being into them.

00:58:06.887 --> 00:58:09.108
But that was kind of like a
normal Sunday school teacher.

00:58:09.108 --> 00:58:11.115
You and I, feet like you
had different tastes-

00:58:11.115 --> 00:58:11.948
Mom.
In women.

00:58:11.948 --> 00:58:13.365
But my point is

00:58:15.267 --> 00:58:16.580
she was a normal Sunday school teacher

00:58:16.580 --> 00:58:18.554
but then Ms. Clark showed up.

00:58:18.554 --> 00:58:19.888
You remember Ms. Clark?

00:58:19.888 --> 00:58:22.123
Oh, okay, now we're
talking, now I remember.

00:58:22.123 --> 00:58:23.790
Ms. Clark, you know,

00:58:23.790 --> 00:58:25.931
had a son who was a
year older than us, Sam.

00:58:25.931 --> 00:58:27.123
Yeah, yeah, yah.

00:58:27.123 --> 00:58:32.123
And her husband was, he was
a science guy at Campbell.

00:58:32.445 --> 00:58:34.200
Well, they lived in that
house that like the,

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:37.125
not the Campbell house but
near the Campbell house

00:58:37.125 --> 00:58:39.551
where like people who were
in town working at Campbell

00:58:39.551 --> 00:58:40.986
would like live for a little bit.

00:58:40.986 --> 00:58:42.449
He was a professor, but he had just...

00:58:42.449 --> 00:58:43.714
They had...

00:58:43.714 --> 00:58:44.989
Yeah, somehow they just moved in

00:58:44.989 --> 00:58:47.089
and they were very associated
with the school and...

00:58:47.089 --> 00:58:50.918
But she was very scholarly
and kind of this like

00:58:50.918 --> 00:58:53.871
homeschool precursor type thing.

00:58:53.871 --> 00:58:55.970
So like when we went to
her Sunday school class

00:58:55.970 --> 00:58:58.058
it wasn't just like, "Hey,
here's a nice Bible story

00:58:58.058 --> 00:59:02.538
and you're gonna have
some prayer and a craft,"

00:59:02.538 --> 00:59:05.121
no, this was intense education.

00:59:06.322 --> 00:59:08.886
Like, "This is what grace
means, this is what mercy means,

00:59:08.886 --> 00:59:11.780
this is how they don't
mean the same thing."

00:59:11.780 --> 00:59:13.983
And it's like posted all over the-

00:59:13.983 --> 00:59:15.756
Yeah, we were being
indoctrinated real hard.

00:59:15.756 --> 00:59:16.701
And it was...

00:59:16.701 --> 00:59:17.565
Yeah, she was...

00:59:17.565 --> 00:59:18.454
Very effectively.
It was very intense.

00:59:18.454 --> 00:59:19.816
Yeah.

00:59:19.816 --> 00:59:21.316
And she was a...

00:59:24.809 --> 00:59:26.010
I wouldn't say a...

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:28.152
How would I describe her?

00:59:28.152 --> 00:59:30.930
Scholarly, intense, proper,

00:59:30.930 --> 00:59:32.380
like prim and proper.

00:59:32.380 --> 00:59:34.857
It was kinda like if she
could have had a ruler

00:59:34.857 --> 00:59:36.390
and smacked you on the hand,

00:59:36.390 --> 00:59:38.728
it was almost like if it was, you know,

00:59:38.728 --> 00:59:40.256
a couple of decades earlier she'd been-

00:59:40.256 --> 00:59:41.089
She was from the old school.

00:59:41.089 --> 00:59:43.436
In that old school vibe.

00:59:43.436 --> 00:59:45.639
And again, this is just Sunday school.

00:59:45.639 --> 00:59:47.092
This is just an elective thing.

00:59:47.092 --> 00:59:48.878
But you know, one of the
things that I think is

00:59:48.878 --> 00:59:51.277
a significant about this

00:59:51.277 --> 00:59:55.522
is that kids respond to
this type of instruction

00:59:55.522 --> 00:59:56.832
and let's just be honest,

00:59:56.832 --> 00:59:59.108
this type of indoctrination
in different ways.

00:59:59.108 --> 01:00:03.002
Some kids sniff it out and
are like, "I'm not into this"

01:00:03.002 --> 01:00:05.215
and they rebel at that point
and they kind of check out,

01:00:05.215 --> 01:00:09.111
even though our personalities
were very different-

01:00:09.111 --> 01:00:10.220
Mm-hm.

01:00:10.220 --> 01:00:13.174
We both locked in on this
thing and we're like,

01:00:13.174 --> 01:00:16.101
for whatever reason, this is
it, man, this is important

01:00:16.101 --> 01:00:18.652
like, I got to listen to these people,

01:00:18.652 --> 01:00:20.135
I got to be about this

01:00:20.135 --> 01:00:23.697
and I think that the fact that we both

01:00:23.697 --> 01:00:25.864
latched onto it so hard-

01:00:25.864 --> 01:00:26.869
Really brought us together.

01:00:26.869 --> 01:00:28.424
Is really what made us such good friends.

01:00:28.424 --> 01:00:29.257
Yeah.

01:00:29.257 --> 01:00:30.431
Leading into middle school.

01:00:30.431 --> 01:00:32.773
Even if we weren't in the same class,

01:00:32.773 --> 01:00:36.330
we didn't see each other at
school for every other year.

01:00:36.330 --> 01:00:37.163
Yeah.

01:00:37.163 --> 01:00:39.373
That's absolutely what did it, you know,

01:00:39.373 --> 01:00:41.290
and she was also artsy.

01:00:42.582 --> 01:00:46.665
She didn't write it but
she brought in this play,

01:00:48.808 --> 01:00:51.385
The Bible Tells Me So Show.

01:00:51.385 --> 01:00:52.961
Mm, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:00:52.961 --> 01:00:54.719
That was her, huh?
Yep.

01:00:54.719 --> 01:00:58.838
Her son, Sam was like, a
gifted actor and singer

01:00:58.838 --> 01:01:03.004
and very charismatic and
like larger than life

01:01:03.004 --> 01:01:05.464
but in retrospect I it think was,

01:01:05.464 --> 01:01:10.352
okay, she can't cast her own
son to be the starring role

01:01:10.352 --> 01:01:12.395
of this because-

01:01:12.395 --> 01:01:13.292
The host of the show.

01:01:13.292 --> 01:01:14.947
You know, it was The
Bible Tells Me So Show-

01:01:14.947 --> 01:01:15.875
It feels like a game show.

01:01:15.875 --> 01:01:18.748
It was a spoof of Family Feud

01:01:18.748 --> 01:01:21.600
if Family Feud was just
about Bible trivia.

01:01:21.600 --> 01:01:22.433
Yeah.

01:01:22.433 --> 01:01:24.992
And then the two teams that competed that

01:01:24.992 --> 01:01:28.909
which like the two families
were friend groups.

01:01:29.807 --> 01:01:31.470
So it's like all the...

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:33.090
They were like all kids in a youth group

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:36.266
that knew each other, so
there was like some sort...

01:01:36.266 --> 01:01:37.347
I wouldn't call it drama

01:01:37.347 --> 01:01:38.954
but there was interplay,

01:01:38.954 --> 01:01:40.634
friendship interplay between them.

01:01:40.634 --> 01:01:41.475
As part of the story.

01:01:41.475 --> 01:01:42.751
Christian interplay.

01:01:42.751 --> 01:01:43.584
As part of the story.

01:01:43.584 --> 01:01:46.425
As part of the story that
rounded out it being-

01:01:46.425 --> 01:01:48.130
'Cause we were all in
the same youth group.

01:01:48.130 --> 01:01:48.963
Yeah.

01:01:48.963 --> 01:01:50.920
So she couldn't cast her
own son to be the star,

01:01:50.920 --> 01:01:53.229
which he clearly needed to be.

01:01:53.229 --> 01:01:54.062
Yeah.

01:01:54.062 --> 01:01:55.918
As I recall, that would be the case.

01:01:57.581 --> 01:02:01.495
And so she cast me to be the host of

01:02:01.495 --> 01:02:04.378
The Bible Tells Me So Show.

01:02:04.378 --> 01:02:07.480
I remember you getting that part,

01:02:07.480 --> 01:02:12.010
and this was at a time that I
was like any sort of like...

01:02:12.010 --> 01:02:15.715
I was about being a class
clown and like, you know,

01:02:15.715 --> 01:02:18.369
cutting up and getting
attention in that way.

01:02:18.369 --> 01:02:19.202
I was too.

01:02:19.202 --> 01:02:20.230
And you were too

01:02:20.230 --> 01:02:21.441
but when it came to like,

01:02:21.441 --> 01:02:23.884
this is organized and
somebody else is dictating

01:02:23.884 --> 01:02:27.023
what this is I was always
like, "This is not for me"

01:02:27.023 --> 01:02:27.856
but then-

01:02:27.856 --> 01:02:28.689
Well, especially because it was a play.

01:02:28.689 --> 01:02:29.522
But yeah, yeah.

01:02:29.522 --> 01:02:30.355
Like, I had this-

01:02:30.355 --> 01:02:31.695
We didn't think we were the drama kids.

01:02:31.695 --> 01:02:32.528
Right, right.

01:02:32.528 --> 01:02:35.771
But I remember you getting
cast as that and it's like,

01:02:35.771 --> 01:02:38.421
now I'm thinking back to what
I must have been thinking

01:02:38.421 --> 01:02:39.834
and be like, well, you know what?

01:02:39.834 --> 01:02:41.225
He is very clean.

01:02:42.216 --> 01:02:43.049
Like a host, like a TV host.

01:02:43.049 --> 01:02:45.222
Yeah, he's like, he's very put together.

01:02:45.222 --> 01:02:47.183
Like, this is good casting.

01:02:48.332 --> 01:02:49.998
You know what I'm saying?

01:02:49.998 --> 01:02:51.133
You're not gonna give
the kid with the buzz

01:02:51.133 --> 01:02:53.762
the hosting job 'cause it's
like, you had like hair,

01:02:53.762 --> 01:02:56.348
like you had hair that
could be like combed into a-

01:02:56.348 --> 01:02:59.435
And at Easter I would
wear suits to church-

01:02:59.435 --> 01:03:01.029
Yeah, yeah.
So I had a few suits.

01:03:01.029 --> 01:03:01.862
You had, yeah.

01:03:01.862 --> 01:03:03.385
You had a blazer, at least one.

01:03:03.385 --> 01:03:04.222
Yeah.

01:03:04.222 --> 01:03:05.562
Right, yeah.

01:03:05.562 --> 01:03:07.286
I think it was pretty good casting.

01:03:07.286 --> 01:03:10.991
I think Sam would've
taken it to gone too hard.

01:03:10.991 --> 01:03:12.374
He def would've gone too hard.

01:03:12.374 --> 01:03:13.768
He went so hard at everything.

01:03:14.772 --> 01:03:17.412
Yeah, so, I mean, you were in the play.

01:03:17.412 --> 01:03:18.460
I was on one of the teams.

01:03:18.460 --> 01:03:19.976
All the youth group were there

01:03:19.976 --> 01:03:20.809
but I had so-

01:03:20.809 --> 01:03:21.940
Good answer, good answer.

01:03:21.940 --> 01:03:22.872
I had so many lines.

01:03:22.872 --> 01:03:24.417
That was one of my lines.

01:03:24.417 --> 01:03:26.722
And it was a musical.

01:03:26.722 --> 01:03:29.263
♪ When I see trees ♪

01:03:29.263 --> 01:03:30.096
Hold on.

01:03:30.096 --> 01:03:31.317
♪ Waving in the breeze. ♪

01:03:31.317 --> 01:03:32.232
You had to sing that?

01:03:32.232 --> 01:03:35.140
♪ And hear the birds in the air ♪

01:03:35.140 --> 01:03:38.458
♪ I thank the Lord for all
that he has done this. ♪

01:03:38.458 --> 01:03:39.291
It's amazing that you remember this.

01:03:39.291 --> 01:03:42.581
♪ To show me he cares ♪

01:03:42.581 --> 01:03:43.661
You don't remember that?

01:03:43.661 --> 01:03:44.501
No, man.

01:03:44.501 --> 01:03:45.881
I don't remember that, dude.

01:03:45.881 --> 01:03:48.489
♪ Oh, give thanks to the Lord ♪

01:03:48.489 --> 01:03:50.288
♪ For he is good to all ♪

01:03:50.288 --> 01:03:51.340
This is why there's two of us.

01:03:51.340 --> 01:03:53.097
I remember certain things
and remembers other things

01:03:53.097 --> 01:03:55.818
♪ He sends the sun to light our day ♪

01:03:55.818 --> 01:03:57.491
♪ And helps the rain to fall ♪

01:03:57.491 --> 01:03:58.324
No, hold on.

01:03:58.324 --> 01:03:59.415
Do you think you could
sing this without the fact

01:03:59.415 --> 01:04:00.464
that you found it on video?

01:04:00.464 --> 01:04:03.166
I found the video, I didn't watch it.

01:04:03.166 --> 01:04:06.068
I remember this stuff from
then absofreakinlutely.

01:04:06.068 --> 01:04:06.901
Really?

01:04:06.901 --> 01:04:07.734
The thing that I-

01:04:07.734 --> 01:04:08.567
It's crazy.

01:04:08.567 --> 01:04:09.915
And what I wanna do is,

01:04:09.915 --> 01:04:11.998
I want to digitize the...

01:04:13.063 --> 01:04:16.394
I've got this VHS, it's got
my mom's handwriting on it,

01:04:16.394 --> 01:04:18.522
she kept it all these
years and now I have it.

01:04:18.522 --> 01:04:20.285
Let's put that-
I wanna digitize

01:04:20.285 --> 01:04:22.055
The Bible tells me so show.
On the society.

01:04:22.055 --> 01:04:22.888
Let's put it on the
society and we'll like,

01:04:22.888 --> 01:04:23.773
do a director's commentary...

01:04:23.773 --> 01:04:25.348
Well, we didn't direct it.

01:04:25.348 --> 01:04:27.940
We'll get Ms. Clark to do it.

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:29.060
I love Ms. Clark.

01:04:29.060 --> 01:04:32.816
No, we'll do a participant
and actor's commentary

01:04:32.816 --> 01:04:34.522
over this thing.

01:04:34.522 --> 01:04:35.681
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:04:35.681 --> 01:04:37.092
This is a really good idea.

01:04:37.092 --> 01:04:39.488
I think we kind of have to
put it on the Mythical Society

01:04:39.488 --> 01:04:41.572
just because that's where
it makes the most sense

01:04:41.572 --> 01:04:44.390
and there might be
rights issues otherwise.

01:04:44.390 --> 01:04:47.514
That's what the Mythical
Society is all about, man.

01:04:47.514 --> 01:04:48.347
Yeah.

01:04:48.347 --> 01:04:49.448
It's about the coolest stuff.

01:04:49.448 --> 01:04:51.436
I remember all the
songs, but I'll tell you

01:04:51.436 --> 01:04:54.589
what I don't remember, any of my lines.

01:04:54.589 --> 01:04:57.035
Even at the time, I remember
drilling my lines...

01:04:57.035 --> 01:05:00.452
I had a stack of stuff, I
had so many freaking lines.

01:05:00.452 --> 01:05:02.172
Weren't you at one point in the baptismal?

01:05:02.172 --> 01:05:05.715
Didn't the baptismal open
up and you said something?

01:05:05.715 --> 01:05:07.324
I don't think so.

01:05:07.324 --> 01:05:08.405
In my mind that's what happened.

01:05:08.405 --> 01:05:11.103
I had the script on the pulpit

01:05:11.103 --> 01:05:14.381
and I would flip the script
and I'd follow it along.

01:05:14.381 --> 01:05:15.390
I couldn't memorize all the lines.

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:17.378
Did we do that on a Sunday morning?

01:05:17.378 --> 01:05:18.874
Did they give us the service?

01:05:18.874 --> 01:05:20.707
I don't know, maybe.

01:05:21.743 --> 01:05:23.623
Or was it like it come to
this thing on Wednesday night.

01:05:23.623 --> 01:05:25.510
But it was such, you know, there was...

01:05:25.510 --> 01:05:27.398
We were so...

01:05:27.398 --> 01:05:30.872
Like, the youth group was
like so much of our identity

01:05:30.872 --> 01:05:34.119
and like you said, it
really brought us together

01:05:34.119 --> 01:05:37.565
and it was like our youth
group against the world,

01:05:37.565 --> 01:05:38.418
against...

01:05:38.418 --> 01:05:40.839
Like, when we went in they
would talk in terms of like,

01:05:40.839 --> 01:05:43.495
when you go into school it's
like you support each other

01:05:43.495 --> 01:05:45.384
and you hold each other accountable

01:05:45.384 --> 01:05:46.675
to the things that we're committed to.

01:05:46.675 --> 01:05:48.203
But we were developing this

01:05:48.203 --> 01:05:51.370
incredibly tribalistic mindset, right.

01:05:52.795 --> 01:05:54.672
And I'm using that

01:05:54.672 --> 01:05:58.927
in terms of how hard you
identify with another group

01:05:58.927 --> 01:06:02.510
of people and an ideology
to the point that

01:06:04.808 --> 01:06:07.566
in this tiny little town

01:06:07.566 --> 01:06:09.818
where everybody pretty
much thought the same stuff

01:06:09.818 --> 01:06:10.869
to begin with.

01:06:10.869 --> 01:06:11.702
Yeah.

01:06:11.702 --> 01:06:13.614
And again, they might think
a little bit differently

01:06:13.614 --> 01:06:14.742
about some things,

01:06:14.742 --> 01:06:19.624
but like we developed this
mindset that we were different

01:06:19.624 --> 01:06:21.756
even in this place that was so different

01:06:21.756 --> 01:06:23.028
from everything else.

01:06:23.028 --> 01:06:24.189
Mm-hm.

01:06:24.189 --> 01:06:27.746
And I just had no idea what
was happening in my mind.

01:06:27.746 --> 01:06:29.038
It's like, that is so...

01:06:29.038 --> 01:06:32.199
I'm so happy I was able to let go of that.

01:06:32.199 --> 01:06:34.739
Well, one of the things
was pride, you know like-

01:06:34.739 --> 01:06:36.267
Just thinking that you're
the rightest of the right

01:06:36.267 --> 01:06:37.555
of the right of the right of the right.

01:06:37.555 --> 01:06:40.212
We definitely took pride in that and said,

01:06:40.212 --> 01:06:42.399
okay, now I can...

01:06:42.399 --> 01:06:45.531
"Through this lens, I
can understand who I am."

01:06:45.531 --> 01:06:47.185
And when you find other people who think

01:06:47.185 --> 01:06:48.908
exactly the same way,

01:06:48.908 --> 01:06:52.309
it creates an incredible bond, you know.

01:06:52.309 --> 01:06:54.299
And we'll talk about middle
school and high school

01:06:54.299 --> 01:06:56.873
in two other episodes.

01:06:56.873 --> 01:06:57.706
Mm-hm.

01:06:57.706 --> 01:06:58.539
But yeah,

01:06:58.539 --> 01:06:59.651
it's like this being established-

01:06:59.651 --> 01:07:00.484
It fuels it.

01:07:00.484 --> 01:07:04.953
Now in sort of that late
elementary school is what...

01:07:04.953 --> 01:07:08.090
It's the reason that we ended
up staying such good friends

01:07:08.090 --> 01:07:09.561
through all that and it's the reason

01:07:09.561 --> 01:07:10.906
that we went to college together,

01:07:10.906 --> 01:07:12.304
it's the reason that we
went on staff together,

01:07:12.304 --> 01:07:14.921
it's the reason that we're
sitting right here at this table.

01:07:14.921 --> 01:07:17.778
So I can be kind of like,
I can poke fun at it

01:07:17.778 --> 01:07:19.781
or I can say that it's toxic in some ways

01:07:19.781 --> 01:07:23.364
but we really owe
everything to the church.

01:07:25.381 --> 01:07:26.214
You know.

01:07:26.214 --> 01:07:28.006
♪ The Bible Tells Me So Show ♪

01:07:28.006 --> 01:07:29.856
♪ Yeah, it's the place to be ♪

01:07:29.856 --> 01:07:30.689
Yeah.

01:07:30.689 --> 01:07:31.522
♪ You'll see. ♪

01:07:31.522 --> 01:07:32.355
It's very catchy.

01:07:32.355 --> 01:07:33.188
Did she write that?

01:07:33.188 --> 01:07:34.590
I don't think she wrote any of it

01:07:34.590 --> 01:07:37.065
but she was capable of it.

01:07:37.065 --> 01:07:38.032
She's very capable.

01:07:38.032 --> 01:07:38.865
I'm a little disappointed

01:07:38.865 --> 01:07:41.172
you don't remember any of the songs.

01:07:41.172 --> 01:07:42.931
I don't know how that's...

01:07:42.931 --> 01:07:44.337
I wish I could repurpose
that part of my brain.

01:07:44.337 --> 01:07:45.170
Dude, I don't remember the words

01:07:45.170 --> 01:07:46.837
to any of our songs.

01:07:48.486 --> 01:07:49.724
When we get ready to do a concert

01:07:49.724 --> 01:07:50.974
I'm like, "Give me a few days,

01:07:52.432 --> 01:07:55.326
I have to reacquaint myself
with every part of this."

01:07:55.326 --> 01:07:56.159
But lines.

01:07:56.159 --> 01:07:57.495
I mean, I don't know.

01:07:57.495 --> 01:07:58.950
It's a different spot in my brain.

01:07:58.950 --> 01:07:59.783
I don't know.

01:07:59.783 --> 01:08:00.616
Yeah.

01:08:00.616 --> 01:08:02.045
Maybe I should be more into musicals now

01:08:02.045 --> 01:08:03.950
and maybe I could have a career in music.

01:08:03.950 --> 01:08:05.210
You know what?

01:08:05.210 --> 01:08:06.120
I thought about this,

01:08:06.120 --> 01:08:09.287
I don't think it's music versus lines,

01:08:11.261 --> 01:08:13.858
I think it's your long-term memory.

01:08:13.858 --> 01:08:15.608
There's a part of your
brain that stores it

01:08:15.608 --> 01:08:16.657
really, really effectively.

01:08:16.657 --> 01:08:17.490
Yeah.

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:19.713
I have a really good short-term memory.

01:08:19.713 --> 01:08:21.095
I have very bad long-term memory.

01:08:21.095 --> 01:08:21.928
Mm-mm.

01:08:21.928 --> 01:08:24.067
In terms of like lyrics like that.

01:08:24.067 --> 01:08:27.682
So I do think this sets
up our next conversation,

01:08:27.682 --> 01:08:30.730
you know, when we revisit this
and go into middle school.

01:08:30.730 --> 01:08:32.102
I mean, because there's so...

01:08:32.102 --> 01:08:33.968
You know, there were so many pressures

01:08:33.968 --> 01:08:36.318
that then of puberty and-

01:08:36.318 --> 01:08:37.661
A lot of pressure.

01:08:37.661 --> 01:08:38.680
And dating.

01:08:38.680 --> 01:08:39.513
Yeah.

01:08:39.513 --> 01:08:40.346
Pressure in the pants.

01:08:41.646 --> 01:08:42.479
Better pop that Zipper open.

01:08:44.842 --> 01:08:45.675
So,

01:08:46.857 --> 01:08:48.088
but there's different...

01:08:48.088 --> 01:08:49.303
I think there's maybe,

01:08:49.303 --> 01:08:50.607
and we've explored some of that,

01:08:50.607 --> 01:08:52.777
but there's like other ways
to look at middle school.

01:08:52.777 --> 01:08:54.383
Yeah, we're not just
gonna talk about erections

01:08:54.383 --> 01:08:55.216
all the time.

01:08:55.216 --> 01:08:57.440
And high school as we get back into that

01:08:57.440 --> 01:08:59.563
but I think this is a
good start, you know,

01:08:59.563 --> 01:09:03.980
I have a good appreciation
for how it's a combination

01:09:04.932 --> 01:09:06.932
of so many circumstances

01:09:08.274 --> 01:09:11.902
and like, in spite of our differences

01:09:11.902 --> 01:09:13.373
that brought us together,

01:09:13.373 --> 01:09:16.437
it's like we had these things
that mattered the most to us.

01:09:16.437 --> 01:09:17.270
Yeah.

01:09:17.270 --> 01:09:18.818
That brought us together

01:09:18.818 --> 01:09:19.919
and they were the, you know,

01:09:19.919 --> 01:09:23.037
and the amount of time
associated with those things

01:09:23.037 --> 01:09:26.064
and those priorities really worked.

01:09:26.064 --> 01:09:30.753
But I mean, you could be
sitting here with Matt McKinney

01:09:30.753 --> 01:09:34.426
or even more likely, I could be, you know,

01:09:34.426 --> 01:09:35.402
there were times...

01:09:35.402 --> 01:09:37.347
Because like in the youth group

01:09:37.347 --> 01:09:39.689
he was probably the other
guy that like at times,

01:09:39.689 --> 01:09:41.452
like I really hit it
